diff --git a/legacy-origin/README.md b/legacy-origin/README.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..426cb47
--- /dev/null
+++ b/legacy-origin/README.md
@@ -0,0 +1,22 @@
+# Old-origin files (https://dailygrind.example.com)
+
+This repo is now served from **https://app.dailygrind.example.com**. Both origins share the
+registrable domain `example.com`. Web App Origin Migration requires that: the two origins must be same-site.
+
+Deploy these files to the root of the **old** origin:
+
+| File | Purpose |
+| --- | --- |
+| `.well-known/web-app-origin-association` | Lets the new origin take over the installation. Serve it as `application/json`. |
+| `manifest.webmanifest` | Replaces the old manifest. `migrate_to` tells existing installs to migrate right away. |
+| `migration-bridge.html` | Hidden iframe the new app loads to copy saved settings (localStorage) over. |
+| `migration-banner.js` | Fallback banner for browsers without origin migration (Firefox, Safari). |
+
+Notes:
+- Browser support: Chrome/Edge 149+. Elsewhere, users keep the old install and see the banner.
+- Only the installation (shortcuts, icons, OS integration) migrates. Data and permissions don't.
+  `js/import-legacy-settings.js` copies localStorage once. Permissions like notifications have to be granted again.
+- Let only the new origin frame `migration-bridge.html`, by sending this HTTP header:
+  `Content-Security-Policy: frame-ancestors https://app.dailygrind.example.com`
+- To make migration mandatory, add `"behavior": "force"` to the `migrate_from` entry in the
+  new site's `manifest.webmanifest`. Users then have to pick Update or Remove.
